f:["$","path",null,{"d":"M18.901 1.153h3.68L14.54 11.023 24 22.847h-7.406l-5.8-7.585-6.634 7.585H.477l8.6-9.83L0 1.153h7.594l5.243 6.932zm-1.29 19.614h2.039L6.486 3.123h-2.19z"}]
1e:T427,The national security organs are- (a) the Kenya Defence Forces; (b) the National Intelligence Service; and (c) the National Police Service.

The primary object of the national security organs and security system is to promote and guarantee national security in accordance with the principles mentioned in Article 238(2).

In performing their functions and exercising their powers, the national security organs and every member of the national security organs shall not- (a) act in a partisan manner; (b) further any interest of a political party or cause; or (c) prejudice a political interest or political cause that is legitimate under this Constitution.

A person shall not establish a military, paramilitary, or similar organisation that purports to promote and guarantee national security, except as provided for by this Constitution or an Act of Parliament.

The national security organs are subordinate to civilian authority.

Parliament shall enact legislation to provide for the functions, organisation and administration of the national security organs.1f:T59f,There is established a National Security Council.

The Council consists of- (a) the President; (b) the Deputy President; (c) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for defence; (d) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for foreign affairs; (e) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for internal security; (f) the Attorney-General; (g) the Chief of Kenya Defence Forces; (h) the Director-General of the National Intelligence Service; and (i) the Inspector-General of the National Police Service.

The Council shall exercise supervisory control over national security organs and perform any other functions prescribed by national legislation.

The President shall preside at meetings of the Council.

The Council shall appoint its secretary.

The Council shall- (a) integrate the domestic, foreign and military policies relating to national security in order to enable the national security organs to co-operate and function effectively; and (b) assess and appraise the objectives, commitments and risks to the Republic in respect of actual and potential national security capabilities.

The Council shall report annually to Parliament on the state of the security of Kenya.

The Council may, with the approval of Parliament- (a) deploy national forces outside Kenya for- (i) regional or international peace support operations; or (ii) other support operations; and (b) approve the deployment of foreign forces in Kenya. PART 2 - THE KENYA DEFENCE FORCES20:T4e8,There are established the Kenya Defence Forces.

The Defence Forces consist of- (a) the Kenya Army; (b) the Kenya Air Force; and (c) the Kenya Navy.

The Defence Forces- (a) are responsible for the defence and protection of the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Republic; (b) shall assist and cooperate with other authorities in situations of emergency or disaster, and report to the National Assembly whenever deployed in such circumstances; and (c) may be deployed to restore peace in any part of Kenya affected by unrest or instability only with the approval of the National Assembly.

The composition of the command of the Defence Forces shall reflect the regional and ethnic diversity of the people of Kenya.

There is established a Defence Council.

The Council consist of- (a) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for defence, who is the chairperson; (b) the Chief of the Kenya Defence Forces; (c) the three commanders of the defence forces; and (d) the Principal Secretary in the Ministry responsible for defence.

The Council- (a) is responsible for the overall policy, control, and supervision of the Kenya Defence Forces; and (b) performs any other functions prescribed by national legislation. PART 3 - THE NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE SERVICE21:T77a,There is established the office of the Inspector-General of the National Police Service.

The Inspector-General- (a) is appointed by the President with the approval of Parliament; and (b) shall exercise independent command over the National Police Service, and perform any other functions prescribed by national legislation.

The Kenya Police Service and the Administration Police Service shall each be headed by a Deputy Inspector-General appointed by the President in accordance with the recommendation of the National Police Service Commission.

The Cabinet secretary responsible for police services may lawfully give a direction to the Inspector-General with respect to any matter of policy for the National Police Service, but no person may give a direction to the Inspector- General with respect to- (a) the investigation of any particular offence or offences; (b) the enforcement of the law against any particular person or persons; or (c) the employment, assignment, promotion, suspension or dismissal of any member of the National Police Service.

Any direction given to the Inspector-General by the Cabinet secretary responsible for police services under clause (4), or any direction given to the Inspector-General by the Director of Public Prosecutions under Article 157(4), shall be in writing.

The Inspector-General shall be appointed for a single four-year term, and is not eligible for re-appointment.

The Inspector-General may be removed from office by the President only on the grounds of- (a) serious violation of this Constitution or any other law, including a contravention of Chapter Six; (b) gross misconduct whether in the performance of the office holder’s functions or otherwise; (c) physical or mental incapacity to perform the functions of office; (d) incompetence; (e) bankruptcy; or (e) any other just cause.

Parliament shall enact legislation to give full effect to this Article.10:["$","main",null,{"className":"flex-1 max-w-[1180px] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-8 lg:px-12 py-10 sm:py-14 w-full space-y-12","children":[["$","section",null,{"className":"bg-white border border-rule rounded-card overflow-hidden","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"p-6 sm:p-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-4 mb-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-6xl sm:text-[72px] text-flag-green leading-none","children":14}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-2","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans text-xs font-bold text-ink px-2.5 py-1 border border-ink rounded-card","children":"State Organs"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans text-xs font-bold text-ink px-2.5 py-1 border border-ink rounded-card","children":"Articles 238 to 247"}]]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"text-[clamp(32px,4.4vw,56px)] font-black text-ink tracking-[-0.03em] leading-[1.02] font-sans","children":"National Security"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-serif italic text-base sm:text-lg text-ink-muted mt-2","children":["Kiswahili: ","Usalama wa Kitaifa"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] sm:text-[19px] text-ink mt-4 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl","children":"Regulates national security organs: Kenya Defence Forces (KDF), National Intelligence Service (NIS), and National Police Service (NPS). Subjects security forces to civilian authority and human rights standards."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"background":"linear-gradient(to right, #111111 0% 30%, #be1111 30% 50%, #0a5c27 50% 100%)","height":"6px","width":"100%"},"className":"flex-shrink-0 ","aria-hidden":"true"}]]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"p-6 sm:p-8 bg-paper border border-rule border-l-[6px] border-l-flag-green rounded-card","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-sans font-bold text-base sm:text-lg text-ink mb-3","children":"Key constitutional takeaways"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2.5","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"National security shall be pursued in compliance with the law and human rights standards."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Security organs are strictly non-partisan and forbidden from acting for political interests."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"font-serif text-[17px] text-ink leading-relaxed flex items-start gap-2.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-sans font-black text-flag-green text-sm flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5","children":"•"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Creation of the independent National Police Service Commission and IPOA oversight."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-[clamp(28px,3.6vw,44px)] font-black text-ink tracking-[-0.025em] leading-[1.05] font-sans","children":["Articles in Chapter ",14]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-serif text-ink-muted text-base mt-1","children":"Select an article to read its legal clauses and plain language meaning."}]]}],["$","$L1d",null,{"articles":[{"articleNumber":238,"title":"Principles of national security","summary":"Security must promote human dignity, democracy, rule of law, and regional peace.","content":"National security is the protection against internal and external threats to Kenya’s territorial integrity and sovereignty, its people, their rights, freedoms, property, peace, stability and prosperity, and other national interests.\n\nThe national security of Kenya shall be promoted and guaranteed in accordance with the following principles- (a) national security is subject to the authority of this Constitution and Parliament; (b) national security shall be pursued in compliance with the law and with the utmost respect for the rule of law, democracy, human rights and fundamental freedoms; (c) in performing their functions and exercising their powers, national security organs shall respect the diverse culture of the communities within Kenya; and (d) recruitment by the national security organs shall reflect the diversity of the Kenyan people in equitable proportions.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"National security is the protection against internal and external threats to Kenya’s territorial integrity and sovereignty, its people, their rights, freedoms, property, peace, stability and prosperity, and other national interests."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The national security of Kenya shall be promoted and guaranteed in accordance with the following principles- (a) national security is subject to the authority of this Constitution and Parliament; (b) national security shall be pursued in compliance with the law and with the utmost respect for the rule of law, democracy, human rights and fundamental freedoms; (c) in performing their functions and exercising their powers, national security organs shall respect the diverse culture of the communities within Kenya; and (d) recruitment by the national security organs shall reflect the diversity of the Kenyan people in equitable proportions."}],"plainExplanation":"National security operations can never be used as an excuse to suspend fundamental human rights or commit torture."},{"articleNumber":239,"title":"National security organs","summary":"Bars security agencies from partisan political activity or serving private factional interests.","content":"$1e","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The national security organs are- (a) the Kenya Defence Forces; (b) the National Intelligence Service; and (c) the National Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The primary object of the national security organs and security system is to promote and guarantee national security in accordance with the principles mentioned in Article 238(2)."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"In performing their functions and exercising their powers, the national security organs and every member of the national security organs shall not- (a) act in a partisan manner; (b) further any interest of a political party or cause; or (c) prejudice a political interest or political cause that is legitimate under this Constitution."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"A person shall not establish a military, paramilitary, or similar organisation that purports to promote and guarantee national security, except as provided for by this Constitution or an Act of Parliament."},{"clauseNumber":"(5)","text":"The national security organs are subordinate to civilian authority."},{"clauseNumber":"(6)","text":"Parliament shall enact legislation to provide for the functions, organisation and administration of the national security organs."}],"plainExplanation":"Police and intelligence officers cannot be mobilized to harass political opposition or rig elections."},{"articleNumber":240,"title":"Establishment of the National Security Council","content":"$1f","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"There is established a National Security Council."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The Council consists of- (a) the President; (b) the Deputy President; (c) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for defence; (d) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for foreign affairs; (e) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for internal security; (f) the Attorney-General; (g) the Chief of Kenya Defence Forces; (h) the Director-General of the National Intelligence Service; and (i) the Inspector-General of the National Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The Council shall exercise supervisory control over national security organs and perform any other functions prescribed by national legislation."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"The President shall preside at meetings of the Council."},{"clauseNumber":"(5)","text":"The Council shall appoint its secretary."},{"clauseNumber":"(6)","text":"The Council shall- (a) integrate the domestic, foreign and military policies relating to national security in order to enable the national security organs to co-operate and function effectively; and (b) assess and appraise the objectives, commitments and risks to the Republic in respect of actual and potential national security capabilities."},{"clauseNumber":"(7)","text":"The Council shall report annually to Parliament on the state of the security of Kenya."},{"clauseNumber":"(8)","text":"The Council may, with the approval of Parliament- (a) deploy national forces outside Kenya for- (i) regional or international peace support operations; or (ii) other support operations; and (b) approve the deployment of foreign forces in Kenya. PART 2 - THE KENYA DEFENCE FORCES"}]},{"articleNumber":241,"title":"Establishment of Defence Forces and Defence Council","content":"$20","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"There are established the Kenya Defence Forces."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The Defence Forces consist of- (a) the Kenya Army; (b) the Kenya Air Force; and (c) the Kenya Navy."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The Defence Forces- (a) are responsible for the defence and protection of the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Republic; (b) shall assist and cooperate with other authorities in situations of emergency or disaster, and report to the National Assembly whenever deployed in such circumstances; and (c) may be deployed to restore peace in any part of Kenya affected by unrest or instability only with the approval of the National Assembly."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"The composition of the command of the Defence Forces shall reflect the regional and ethnic diversity of the people of Kenya."},{"clauseNumber":"(5)","text":"There is established a Defence Council."},{"clauseNumber":"(6)","text":"The Council consist of- (a) the Cabinet Secretary responsible for defence, who is the chairperson; (b) the Chief of the Kenya Defence Forces; (c) the three commanders of the defence forces; and (d) the Principal Secretary in the Ministry responsible for defence."},{"clauseNumber":"(7)","text":"The Council- (a) is responsible for the overall policy, control, and supervision of the Kenya Defence Forces; and (b) performs any other functions prescribed by national legislation. PART 3 - THE NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE SERVICE"}]},{"articleNumber":242,"title":"Establishment of National Intelligence Service","content":"There is established the National Intelligence Service.\n\nThe National Intelligence Service- (a) is responsible for security intelligence and counter intelligence to enhance national security in accordance with this Constitution; and (b) performs any other functions prescribed by national legislation. PART 4 - THE NATIONAL POLICE SERVICE","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"There is established the National Intelligence Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The National Intelligence Service- (a) is responsible for security intelligence and counter intelligence to enhance national security in accordance with this Constitution; and (b) performs any other functions prescribed by national legislation. PART 4 - THE NATIONAL POLICE SERVICE"}]},{"articleNumber":243,"title":"Establishment of the National Police Service","content":"There is established the National Police Service.\n\nThe National Police Service consists of- (a) the Kenya Police Service; and (b) the Administration Police Service.\n\nThe National Police Service is a national service and shall function throughout Kenya. (4) Parliament shall enact legislation to give full effect to this Article.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"There is established the National Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The National Police Service consists of- (a) the Kenya Police Service; and (b) the Administration Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The National Police Service is a national service and shall function throughout Kenya. (4) Parliament shall enact legislation to give full effect to this Article."}]},{"articleNumber":244,"title":"Objects and functions of the National Police Service","content":"The National Police Service shall- (a) strive for the highest standards of professionalism and discipline among its members; (b) prevent corruption and promote and practice transparency and accountability; (c) comply with constitutional standards of human rights and fundamental freedoms; (d) train staff to the highest possible standards of competence and integrity and to respect human rights and fundamental freedoms and dignity; and (e) foster and promote relationships with the broader society."},{"articleNumber":245,"title":"Command of the National Police Service","content":"$21","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"There is established the office of the Inspector-General of the National Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The Inspector-General- (a) is appointed by the President with the approval of Parliament; and (b) shall exercise independent command over the National Police Service, and perform any other functions prescribed by national legislation."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The Kenya Police Service and the Administration Police Service shall each be headed by a Deputy Inspector-General appointed by the President in accordance with the recommendation of the National Police Service Commission."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"The Cabinet secretary responsible for police services may lawfully give a direction to the Inspector-General with respect to any matter of policy for the National Police Service, but no person may give a direction to the Inspector- General with respect to- (a) the investigation of any particular offence or offences; (b) the enforcement of the law against any particular person or persons; or (c) the employment, assignment, promotion, suspension or dismissal of any member of the National Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(5)","text":"Any direction given to the Inspector-General by the Cabinet secretary responsible for police services under clause (4), or any direction given to the Inspector-General by the Director of Public Prosecutions under Article 157(4), shall be in writing."},{"clauseNumber":"(6)","text":"The Inspector-General shall be appointed for a single four-year term, and is not eligible for re-appointment."},{"clauseNumber":"(7)","text":"The Inspector-General may be removed from office by the President only on the grounds of- (a) serious violation of this Constitution or any other law, including a contravention of Chapter Six; (b) gross misconduct whether in the performance of the office holder’s functions or otherwise; (c) physical or mental incapacity to perform the functions of office; (d) incompetence; (e) bankruptcy; or (e) any other just cause."},{"clauseNumber":"(8)","text":"Parliament shall enact legislation to give full effect to this Article."}]},{"articleNumber":246,"title":"National Police Service Commission","content":"There is established the National Police Service Commission.\n\nThe Commission consists of- (a) the following persons, each appointed by the President (i) a person who is qualified to be appointed as a High Court Judge; (ii) two retired senior police officers; and (iii) three persons of integrity who have served the public with distinction; (b) the Inspector-General of the National Police Service; and (c) both Deputy Inspectors-General of the National Police Service.\n\nThe Commission shall- (a) recruit and appoint persons to hold or act in offices in the service, confirm appointments, and determine promotions and transfers within the National Police Service; (b) observing due process, exercise disciplinary control over and remove persons holding or acting in offices within the Service; and (c) perform any other functions prescribed by national legislation.\n\nThe composition of the National Police Service shall reflect the regional and ethnic diversity of the people of Kenya.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"There is established the National Police Service Commission."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The Commission consists of- (a) the following persons, each appointed by the President (i) a person who is qualified to be appointed as a High Court Judge; (ii) two retired senior police officers; and (iii) three persons of integrity who have served the public with distinction; (b) the Inspector-General of the National Police Service; and (c) both Deputy Inspectors-General of the National Police Service."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The Commission shall- (a) recruit and appoint persons to hold or act in offices in the service, confirm appointments, and determine promotions and transfers within the National Police Service; (b) observing due process, exercise disciplinary control over and remove persons holding or acting in offices within the Service; and (c) perform any other functions prescribed by national legislation."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"The composition of the National Police Service shall reflect the regional and ethnic diversity of the people of Kenya."}]},{"articleNumber":247,"title":"Other police services","content":"Parliament may enact legislation establishing other police services under the supervision of the National Police Service and the command of the Inspector- General of the Service."}],"chapterNumber":14}]]}],"$L22"]}]
